CLI

cmd/weld is the Weld command you run in a terminal. It is the composition root: the only place allowed to join the core (matching, QoS) with the transports (DDS bytes). Libraries stay separate; the binary wires them together.

Commands

The dispatcher (cmd/weld/main.go:74) routes echo, pub, topic, service,
action, doctor, params, and launch, and exits 2 on anything else.

- echo: prints std_msgs/String payloads on a topic until the timeout or interrupt. This is the interop check: if Weld sees the ROS talker, bytes flow. Takes no positional args. --qos-durability selects the requested durability: volatile (default, matches ros2 topic echo against any offer) or transient_local (refuses volatile writers through peer-side RxO, which is how QoS gates prove silence).
- pub: sends one std_msgs/String message on a topic, then exits. The message comes from --text or from one positional arg, so weld pub --text hello and weld pub hello send the same bytes. Giving both fails with exit 2 (flag plus positionals); giving two or more args fails with exit 2.
- topic list: lists topics with counts plus types plus hashes. Takes no positional args. --include-hidden shows hidden underscore topics.
- topic info: shows endpoints plus type hashes plus mismatches for one topic (--topic, default /chatter).
- service call: calls one service with a per-call deadline then exits. Service from --service or one positional, request from --request or one positional. Giving both --request and a positional request fails with exit 2 (flag plus positionals). --deadline defaults to 5s.
- action send: sends one goal and waits for result. Name from --name (or --action alias) or one positional, goal from --goal or positionals. Giving both --goal and positional goals fails with exit 2 (flag plus positionals). --timeout defaults to 60s, --cancel-after cancels mid goal (0 disables).
- doctor: lists entities with topic info plus mismatches plus metrics. Takes no positional args. --check exits 1 when any mismatch exists, 0 when clean.
- params: reads and writes node parameters (--node, default /weld). Verbs: declare, get, set (with --atomic), describe, list, events, dump, load.
- launch: resolves node config and prints the canonical dump (--node, --namespace, plus forwarded ROS args).

All take a topic or service or name (defaults /chatter, none, none) and a domain flag (default from ROS_DOMAIN_ID, fallback 0). An invalid ROS_DOMAIN_ID is reported at startup with exit 2. Give each test run its own domain id so fleets stay apart.

Exit codes

- 0: ok. echo also returns 0 on timeout or interrupt after printing what it saw.
- 1: runtime failure (backend, publish, decode, print). The message goes to stderr, first line only, with a weld: prefix naming the operation, for example weld: take bytes: ... or weld: publish bytes: ....
- 2: usage failure (bad flags, bad topic, bad domain, missing pub text). Flag parse errors go to stderr; -h and --help print help to stdout with exit 0.

Options

- --topic: ROS topic name, must start with /. Default /chatter.
- --domain: DDS domain id, 0 through 101. Defaults to ROS_DOMAIN_ID with fallback 0. A bad value fails fast with exit 2 before any socket opens.
- --timeout (echo only): how long echo runs. Default 30s. 0 runs until interrupt; negative values are rejected with exit 2.
- --text (pub only): message data. One positional arg works instead; giving two or more args fails with exit 2.

ROS_DOMAIN_ID is read once at startup to set the --domain default. A bad value there does not break help output; the failure is deferred to command setup, where it is reported as a usage error.

Counters

Gap and drop counters go to stderr at most once per second so logs stay readable: weld: lost N messages when the backend reports skipped sequence numbers, and weld: dropped N messages when a corrupt or duplicate sample is skipped. Payload text always goes to stdout, one message per line.

Build and run

  cd cmd/weld && GOWORK=off go build -o /tmp/weld ./...
  /tmp/weld echo --domain 42
  /tmp/weld pub --domain 42 --topic /chatter --text hello

GOWORK=off is needed because cmd/weld is its own Go module with a replace directive to the parent (see the comment in cmd/weld/go.mod). The parent go.work covers only the root and sdk, so building the CLI with the workspace enabled would miss the replace.

Current state

Both commands run against the live backend in internal/transports/dds: echo joins discovery and prints what the ROS talker publishes, pub publishes one message and prints sent: <text>. echo declares its SEDP reader through subscribeEchoReader (cmd/weld/main.go:302) with SubscribeWithDurability (cmd/weld/main.go:308). pub waits for SPDP routes: publishWithRouteWait retries PublishBytes on ErrNoRoute every 50 ms up to 5 s (cmd/weld/main.go:47-50, cmd/weld/main.go:478). The Go gates automate the full check on allocated domains with go test ./testbed/unit/... -count=1 for offline checks and go test -tags live ./testbed/live/... -count=1 for live checks: they build the CLI, start a CycloneDDS talker in weld-jazzy, run echo with a timeout, then reverse the direction with weld pub against ros2 topic echo. The checks are testbed/live/ros/roundtrip_test.go:107 plus testbed/live/ros/roundtrip_test.go:160 on pooled domains and testbed/live/gates/gate2_test.go:33 plus testbed/live/gates/gate2_test.go:102 with per-run live IDs (testbed/live/helpers/helpers.go:131). Parallel tests allocate exclusive domains 70 through 101 (testbed/live/helpers/domainpool.go); fixed 42, 43, 44, 47, and 48 stay reserved for canonical, distro, and gateway runs. Only one run per domain and topic at a time; never start the compose stack per run.
